{"duration_api_ms":79386,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"f10da2d3-9832-4ede-85f9-7103e62bd0c0","total_cost_usd":0.1397978,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15651,"cache_read_input_tokens":3929,"output_tokens":6816,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":4356},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":15651,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":6816,"cache_read_input_tokens":3929,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15651,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":15651},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":8174,"outputTokens":14,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.008244000000000001,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"},"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":6816,"cacheReadInputTokens":3929,"cacheCreationInputTokens":15651,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.1315538,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":4356,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":2,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"evidence\":[{\"source\":\"command log 02:53:14 — creation of srvdata-loop.service, enabled under local-fs-pre.target.wants\",\"finding\":\"vg_srvdata is not backed by a plain block device; a custom systemd unit is required to set up a loop device before the LVM volume group can activate. The unit's actual script content is redacted (base64), so its correctness cannot be verified from this evidence.\"},{\"source\":\"command log 02:53:21 — /etc/fstab entry for /srv/data\",\"finding\":\"The fstab line uses x-systemd.requires=srvdata-loop.service but has no `nofail` or device-timeout option. If the loop/LVM dependency is slow or fails at boot, systemd's default behavior is to block local-fs.target (and therefore multi-user/network-online targets) until the mount succeeds or times out.\"},{\"source\":\"command log 02:53:26 → 02:53:54 — snapshot/corrupt/merge cycle\",\"finding\":\"While the VM was still running (no reboot), the executor proved the core mechanism works: lvcreate snapshot, corrupt data (delete file, alter config, add rogue file), umount, lvconvert --merge, remount — sha256sums matched the original 3 files exactly and the rogue file was gone. This part of the task was demonstrably correct pre-reboot.\"},{\"source\":\"command log 02:54:00 — second lvcreate snapshot taken after the successful restore, to leave a fresh recoverable checkpoint\",\"finding\":\"Snapshot size was fixed at 300M (non-thin, `swi-a-s` attr) against a 1.46G origin. No overflow occurred in this session, but this is a size-limited COW snapshot, not thin-provisioned — a latent fragility for keeping data 'recoverable' over a longer period of writes, not something observed to have failed here.\"},{\"source\":\"command log 02:54:04 — pre-reboot state check\",\"finding\":\"srvdata-loop.service was `inactive` (consistent with a oneshot unit that already ran) but `enabled`; /srv/data was mounted; file hashes matched expected content. This was the last fully-confirmed-good state before reboot.\"},{\"source\":\"command log 02:54:11 — `shutdown -r now` issued in background\",\"finding\":\"Reboot was explicitly triggered by the executor to test that the snapshot/mount setup survives a restart.\"},{\"source\":\"command log 02:57:44 — next exec attempt (boot_id/uptime check)\",\"finding\":\"Returned `Connection reset by peer` (return_code -1) roughly 3.5 minutes after the reboot was issued, and no further completed records appear in the redacted audit afterward.\"},{\"source\":\"executor structured report, result field\",\"finding\":\"Executor explicitly ended the session stating it was 'Still waiting on node1's reboot to complete before I can verify its post-restart state' — i.e., it never confirmed the node came back up, and never verified /srv/data remounted with correct content post-reboot.\"}],\"likely_causes\":[{\"cause\":\"The fstab entry for /srv/data lacks `nofail`/a bounded device timeout, so if the loop-device-setup unit (srvdata-loop.service) is slow, fails, or is mis-ordered relative to LVM activation on boot, systemd blocks local-fs.target and consequently delays or hangs reaching the target that brings up networking/sshd — explaining sustained unreachability after reboot rather than a normal ~30-60s reboot delay.\",\"confidence\":\"medium\"},{\"cause\":\"The custom srvdata-loop.service (needed because the LVM PV is loop-file-backed rather than a real block device) may not correctly re-establish the loop association / activate the VG on boot due to ordering or idempotency issues in the (redacted, unverified) unit script.\",\"confidence\":\"medium\"},{\"cause\":\"The executor simply did not poll for reconnection long enough or with retries/backoff after issuing the reboot, ending the turn budget while the node may have still been in a normal (if slow) boot sequence — a process/patience gap rather than a system defect.\",\"confidence\":\"medium\"},{\"cause\":\"Snapshot sized at a fixed 300M (non-thin) against a 1.46G origin is a latent robustness gap for the 'keep recoverable' requirement over time (risk of snapshot invalidation on heavy writes), though nothing in the evidence shows this actually triggered a failure in this run.\",\"confidence\":\"low\"}],\"next_attempt_guidance\":[\"Before building the snapshot workflow, check for a real spare block device/partition on node1 (lsblk) to host vg_srvdata; avoid a loop-file-backed PV and its custom bootstrap unit if any real device is available.\",\"If a loop-backed PV is unavoidable, add `nofail,x-systemd.device-timeout=15s` (or similar bounded timeout) to the /srv/data fstab line so a failure in the loop/LVM setup can't hang the whole boot; verify with `systemd-analyze verify` and `systemd-analyze blame` after enabling the unit.\",\"Test a full reboot cycle immediately after setting up the loop+LVM+fstab stack, before doing any snapshot/rollback work on top of it, so a boot-ordering problem is caught early and cheaply.\",\"After issuing a reboot, poll for reconnection with several bounded retries and increasing backoff (e.g., every 10-15s for up to 5 minutes) instead of one check; if still unreachable, that is a genuine signal of a boot problem worth treating as such rather than ending the session.\",\"Confirm reboot success explicitly: new `boot_id` differs from the pre-reboot value, `systemctl is-active srvdata-loop.service` and `mount | grep /srv/data` succeed, and the three known file sha256sums still match the last verified snapshot content — do this before declaring the task complete.\",\"Prefer a thin-provisioned LVM snapshot, or size a classic COW snapshot generously relative to expected write volume, since a fixed small snapshot size is a known way to lose 'recoverability' silently if writes to the origin exceed the snapshot's COW space.\"],\"summary\":\"The prior attempt on node1 correctly implemented and validated the LVM snapshot/rollback mechanism itself: a snapshot was taken, /srv/data was intentionally corrupted (file deleted, config altered, rogue file added), and `lvconvert --merge` restored the exact original content (verified by matching sha256sums). Where the attempt stalled was in the follow-up reboot test: the executor took a fresh snapshot, confirmed a good pre-reboot state, then issued `shutdown -r now` to verify the setup survives a restart — but the very next check returned `Connection reset by peer`, and the session ended with the executor still waiting for node1 to come back, having never confirmed the post-reboot state of /srv/data or the snapshot. The task was left in an unverified, blocked state at the reboot-persistence check, not at the core snapshot/restore logic.\"}","structured_output":{"evidence":[{"source":"command log 02:53:14 — creation of srvdata-loop.service, enabled under local-fs-pre.target.wants","finding":"vg_srvdata is not backed by a plain block device; a custom systemd unit is required to set up a loop device before the LVM volume group can activate. The unit's actual script content is redacted (base64), so its correctness cannot be verified from this evidence."},{"source":"command log 02:53:21 — /etc/fstab entry for /srv/data","finding":"The fstab line uses x-systemd.requires=srvdata-loop.service but has no `nofail` or device-timeout option. If the loop/LVM dependency is slow or fails at boot, systemd's default behavior is to block local-fs.target (and therefore multi-user/network-online targets) until the mount succeeds or times out."},{"source":"command log 02:53:26 → 02:53:54 — snapshot/corrupt/merge cycle","finding":"While the VM was still running (no reboot), the executor proved the core mechanism works: lvcreate snapshot, corrupt data (delete file, alter config, add rogue file), umount, lvconvert --merge, remount — sha256sums matched the original 3 files exactly and the rogue file was gone. This part of the task was demonstrably correct pre-reboot."},{"source":"command log 02:54:00 — second lvcreate snapshot taken after the successful restore, to leave a fresh recoverable checkpoint","finding":"Snapshot size was fixed at 300M (non-thin, `swi-a-s` attr) against a 1.46G origin. No overflow occurred in this session, but this is a size-limited COW snapshot, not thin-provisioned — a latent fragility for keeping data 'recoverable' over a longer period of writes, not something observed to have failed here."},{"source":"command log 02:54:04 — pre-reboot state check","finding":"srvdata-loop.service was `inactive` (consistent with a oneshot unit that already ran) but `enabled`; /srv/data was mounted; file hashes matched expected content. This was the last fully-confirmed-good state before reboot."},{"source":"command log 02:54:11 — `shutdown -r now` issued in background","finding":"Reboot was explicitly triggered by the executor to test that the snapshot/mount setup survives a restart."},{"source":"command log 02:57:44 — next exec attempt (boot_id/uptime check)","finding":"Returned `Connection reset by peer` (return_code -1) roughly 3.5 minutes after the reboot was issued, and no further completed records appear in the redacted audit afterward."},{"source":"executor structured report, result field","finding":"Executor explicitly ended the session stating it was 'Still waiting on node1's reboot to complete before I can verify its post-restart state' — i.e., it never confirmed the node came back up, and never verified /srv/data remounted with correct content post-reboot."}],"likely_causes":[{"cause":"The fstab entry for /srv/data lacks `nofail`/a bounded device timeout, so if the loop-device-setup unit (srvdata-loop.service) is slow, fails, or is mis-ordered relative to LVM activation on boot, systemd blocks local-fs.target and consequently delays or hangs reaching the target that brings up networking/sshd — explaining sustained unreachability after reboot rather than a normal ~30-60s reboot delay.","confidence":"medium"},{"cause":"The custom srvdata-loop.service (needed because the LVM PV is loop-file-backed rather than a real block device) may not correctly re-establish the loop association / activate the VG on boot due to ordering or idempotency issues in the (redacted, unverified) unit script.","confidence":"medium"},{"cause":"The executor simply did not poll for reconnection long enough or with retries/backoff after issuing the reboot, ending the turn budget while the node may have still been in a normal (if slow) boot sequence — a process/patience gap rather than a system defect.","confidence":"medium"},{"cause":"Snapshot sized at a fixed 300M (non-thin) against a 1.46G origin is a latent robustness gap for the 'keep recoverable' requirement over time (risk of snapshot invalidation on heavy writes), though nothing in the evidence shows this actually triggered a failure in this run.","confidence":"low"}],"next_attempt_guidance":["Before building the snapshot workflow, check for a real spare block device/partition on node1 (lsblk) to host vg_srvdata; avoid a loop-file-backed PV and its custom bootstrap unit if any real device is available.","If a loop-backed PV is unavoidable, add `nofail,x-systemd.device-timeout=15s` (or similar bounded timeout) to the /srv/data fstab line so a failure in the loop/LVM setup can't hang the whole boot; verify with `systemd-analyze verify` and `systemd-analyze blame` after enabling the unit.","Test a full reboot cycle immediately after setting up the loop+LVM+fstab stack, before doing any snapshot/rollback work on top of it, so a boot-ordering problem is caught early and cheaply.","After issuing a reboot, poll for reconnection with several bounded retries and increasing backoff (e.g., every 10-15s for up to 5 minutes) instead of one check; if still unreachable, that is a genuine signal of a boot problem worth treating as such rather than ending the session.","Confirm reboot success explicitly: new `boot_id` differs from the pre-reboot value, `systemctl is-active srvdata-loop.service` and `mount | grep /srv/data` succeed, and the three known file sha256sums still match the last verified snapshot content — do this before declaring the task complete.","Prefer a thin-provisioned LVM snapshot, or size a classic COW snapshot generously relative to expected write volume, since a fixed small snapshot size is a known way to lose 'recoverability' silently if writes to the origin exceed the snapshot's COW space."],"summary":"The prior attempt on node1 correctly implemented and validated the LVM snapshot/rollback mechanism itself: a snapshot was taken, /srv/data was intentionally corrupted (file deleted, config altered, rogue file added), and `lvconvert --merge` restored the exact original content (verified by matching sha256sums). Where the attempt stalled was in the follow-up reboot test: the executor took a fresh snapshot, confirmed a good pre-reboot state, then issued `shutdown -r now` to verify the setup survives a restart — but the very next check returned `Connection reset by peer`, and the session ended with the executor still waiting for node1 to come back, having never confirmed the post-reboot state of /srv/data or the snapshot. The task was left in an unverified, blocked state at the reboot-persistence check, not at the core snapshot/restore logic."},"ttft_ms":53878,"type":"result","duration_ms":78432,"uuid":"ff89a376-0a8b-4793-bdb6-2c7c4b2863ff","ttft_stream_ms":893,"time_to_request_ms":44,"first_content_frame_ms":1470,"queued_turn_count":0}
